      <h2>In-Depth Carrier Analysis: Who Should Choose Whom?</h2>

      <h3>1. Erie Insurance: Best Overall for Low Rates & Rate Lock</h3>
      <p>
        Sold across 12 Mid-Atlantic and Midwestern states (including PA, OH, VA, NC, MD, and IL), Erie Insurance is tough to beat on price. Its signature <strong>Erie Rate Lock®</strong> option freezes your rate until you change cars, add or drop a driver, or move to a new address. Even an accident claim won't trigger a surprise hike on its own.
      </p>

      <h3>2. GEICO: Best Cheap Rates for Clean Drivers & Tech Convenience</h3>
      <p>
        GEICO sells direct instead of paying commissions to a massive network of local storefronts, and those lower overhead costs show up in clean-driver quotes. Federal workers, military members, and members of more than 800 alumni and professional groups can also stack affinity discounts of up to 15%.
      </p>

      <h3>3. State Farm: Best Cheap Rates for Drivers with Moving Violations</h3>
      <p>
        State Farm costs a bit more than GEICO if your record is spotless, but it is noticeably more forgiving after a speeding ticket or minor fender bender. Families adding a teen driver should also price out State Farm's <em>Steer Clear®</em> program, which often produces the lowest combined household bill.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Progressive: Best for High-Risk Drivers & Instant Budget Matching</h3>
      <p>
        Progressive built its reputation pricing drivers other companies turn away. Need an SR-22 filing after a suspension or major ticket? Progressive's specialty division is far more likely to quote you a workable rate than a conservative mutual insurer. Its <em>Name Your Price®</em> tool also lets you dial coverage up or down to fit a strict monthly budget.
      </p>

      <h2>The Dangerous "Cheap Insurance" Traps to Avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Chasing the lowest monthly bill is smart, as long as you don't strip away protection you actually need. Before you drop optional coverages, review how liability limits and deductibles work in our <a href="/guides/auto-insurance/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">2026 car insurance cost benchmark</a>:
      </p>

      <div class="card" style="margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <h4 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 0.5rem;">Three Costly Underinsurance Traps:</h4>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Buying Only State Statutory Minimums:</strong> Cause a chain-reaction crash that totals two $45,000 EVs, and a $15,000 or $25,000 state minimum property limit leaves you personally liable for the other $65,000. That puts your paycheck and home equity in the crosshairs. Check your state's legal floor in our <a href="/guides/state-car-insurance-averages/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">50-state auto insurance ranking</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Rejecting Uninsured Motorist Coverage:</strong> Skipping UM/UIM only saves about $4 to $8 a month. If an uninsured driver hits you and takes off, you're left paying your own medical bills and repairs.</li>
          <li><strong>Ignoring Credit-Based Insurance Surcharges:</strong> Drivers with poor credit often pay 70% to 114% more for the exact same policy. See how to move up a tier in our <a href="/guides/insurance-score-factors/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">credit-based insurance score guide</a>.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

